"Describes the functional job a person is trying to accomplish through solution-independent steps from preparation to conclusion.\n\nUse when: Find unmet needs beyond one product's current touchpoints. Investigate opportunities across the complete job rather than one feature.\n\nAvoid when: The goal is documenting the provider's current workflow or a user's interface navigation.\n\nVisual grammar: Ordered job steps describe intended accomplishments with verb-object labels. Universal stages guide coverage from define and locate through execute and conclude. Outcome statements may attach to steps without becoming solutions.\n\nBuild: Define the job executor and core functional job. Identify the accomplishments needed to complete that job independent of tools. Review the sequence and collect desired outcome evidence at relevant steps.\n\nCheck: Steps express what people seek to accomplish rather than current tools. The job boundary is coherent and solution-independent. Outcomes are distinct from proposed features.\n\nPitfalls: Copying existing product screens into the job sequence. Assuming execution alone represents the complete customer job.\n\nExample: A prepare-a-meal job includes deciding needs, locating ingredients, preparing, cooking, monitoring, adjusting and concluding, without prescribing an appliance.\n\nAlternatives: Experience map, Value Proposition Canvas."
